L001498	\section{Space profile of the wave and differences with tygers}
L001499	
L001500	There is a wide literature on thermalization of the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation, expecially with respect to one of the typical observed phenomenon, called tygers. Given $K>0$, the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation is the equation
L001501	\begin{equation}\label{eq:TruncatedBurgers}
L001502	    \partial_t u - P_{K}\left(u\partial_x u\right)=0 \, ,
L001503	\end{equation}
L001504	where $P_K$ is the Fourier projector on the modes $\{-K,-K+1, \cdots,K-1,K\}$. Such an equation has very interesting features that can be summarized as
L001505	\begin{itemize}
L001506	    \item It is conservative and, in particular, it conserves both \emph{momentum} $M$ and \emph{energy} $E$ defined as
L001507	    \begin{eqnarray}
L001508	        M&:=&  \int_0^1 u(x) \, dx \, \\
L001509	        E&:=&\frac{1}{2} \int_0^1 u^2(x) \, dx \, .
L001510	    \end{eqnarray}
L001511	    See, for example, \cite{Majda2000}.
L001512	    \item The equation has a family of invariant Gibbs measures labeled by a parameter $\beta>0$ which plays the role of temperature (see eq.~9 in \cite{Majda2000}).
L001513	    \item As a consequence of Galerkin truncation, short before the shock time there appears rapid oscillations called \emph{tygers} (see \cite{Ray2011}). Those tygers appear shortly before the shock time, at a time $t_{G} \sim t_{s}-K^{-\frac{2}{3}}$ (see eq.~(B5) in \cite{Ray2011}).
L001514	    \item When the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation is taken with the minus sign in front of the nonliearity, as it happens for the field $\lambda$ in eq.~\eqref{eq:Burgerissime}, is is known that the tygers appear in the descending profile of $u$ (see Fig.~1 in \cite{Ray2011}, Fig.~1 and the discussion at page 399 in \cite{Ray2015}).
L001515	    \item At late times (estimeed in \cite{Venkataraman2017}) the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation exhibits thermalization (see e.g.~Fig~1 in \cite{Ray2015}).
L001516	\end{itemize}
L001517	Given this short account on the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation, let us briefly discuss the differences and analogy with respect to the system of inviscid Burgers equations \ref{eq:Burgerissime} obtained for the renormalized fields $\lambda,\rho$ in FPUT.
L001518	
L001519	Indeed, the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation is the first order normal form of the FPUT model (compare e.g.~eq.~(29) in \cite{Benettin2008-1D} and eq.~(5) in \cite{Majda2000}. Being the normal form, up to the reminder, means explicitly that the FPUT equations of motion can be written as
L001520	\begin{equation}\label{eq:DoppiaKDV}
L001521	    \begin{split}
L001522	        \lambda_\tau &= \left(1+\frac{\alpha \sqrt{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{2}} \lambda \right) \lambda_x + \mathcal{R}(\lambda,\rho) \\
L001523	        \rho_\tau&=-\left(1+\frac{\alpha \sqrt{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{2}} \rho\right)\rho_x - \mathcal{R}(\rho,\lambda)
L001524	    \end{split}
L001525	\end{equation}
L001526	where, for $\epsilon$ small, the leading terms in $\mathcal{R}$ yields the pair of counterpropagating Korteweg-de Vries equations (see e.g.~Eq.~(93) in \cite{Gallone2022} or, more explicitly, equation (6.69) in \cite{Gallone-Review-2025} (The coefficient in front of $\lambda_{xxx}$ is correct in \eqref{eq:DoppiaKDV})
L001527	\begin{equation}
L001528	    \begin{split}
L001529	        \lambda_\tau &= \left(1+\frac{\alpha \sqrt{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{2}} \lambda \right) \lambda_x +\frac{1}{24 \,N^2} \lambda_{xxx}+\widetilde{\mathcal{R}}(\lambda,\rho) \\
L001530	        \rho_\tau&=-\left(1+\frac{\alpha \sqrt{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{2}} \rho\right)\rho_x -\frac{1}{24 \,N^2} \rho_{xxx} - \widetilde{\mathcal{R}}(\rho,\lambda)
L001531	    \end{split}
L001532	\end{equation}
L001533	where, for any finite lattice, the regularisation observed is due to the small (but finite, when $N$ is finite) dispersion. Therefore, the dynamics of FPUT -- at the level of equations -- is deeply different from the one of the Galerkin truncated Burgers equation \emph{close} and \emph{after} the shock time. The short wavelength oscillations observed in the FPUT system are due to the small dispersion and, in fact, behave also qualitatively differently from the tygers (see, for example, Fig.~\ref{fig:SpatialPlot}).
L001534	
L001535	\begin{figure}[h!]
L001536	    \begin{center}
L001537	        \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{SawTooth.pdf}
L001538	        \caption{Space profile of the solution of the FPUT system at the shock time and after the shock time. $N=4096$, initial solution with only one mode initially excited with $R=0$ at $t=0$.}\label{fig:SpatialPlot}
L001539	    \end{center}
L001540	\end{figure}
L001541	Indeed, differently from the tygers which would have appeared on the descending part of the space profile, our short wavelength oscillations appear close to the point of the gradient catastrophe.
L001542	
L001543	Having clarified the differences among the two phenomena we would like also to stress similarities, in the \emph{thermalization} phenomenon. For small specific energy, the approach to thermal equilibrium suggested by the truncated Burgers equation is different from the one of the FPUT (this is noted already in \cite{Ray2011}, where the authors write: ``It must be noted that the mechanism which prevents thermalization in the Fermi-Pasta-Ulam problem does not seem to be present here''). Indeed, the route to thermalization must take into account both the dispersion term and all \emph{nonintegrable} terms present in $\widetilde{\mathcal{R}}$.
L001544	
L001545	Nevertheless, when the specific energy is large enough, the thermalization of the FPUT chain is qualitatively similar to the one of the truncated Burgers equation. Indeed, after the shock time, the Fourier Energy Spectrum of the FPUT chain exhibits a stable power law $E_k\sim k^{-2}$ in an inertial range that deteriorates with time (see Fig.~2 and 6 in \cite{Gallone2024}). Correspondingly, in the space profile of the wave, the bunch of short wavelength oscillations formed close to the shock expands until it covers the whole profile. This phenomenon, which is qualitatively described in the concluding part of \cite{Gallone2022-PRL}, is qualitatively similar to what happens with tygers (see e.g.~Fig.~1 in \cite{Avellaneda1995-2}).
