L001676	\section{Some details on the numerics}
L001677	In this Section, we present an example of FES and explain how the shock time is computed numerically.
L001678	
L001679	We integrate the FPUT system using a fourth-order Yoshida algorithm. Since this algorithm is symplectic, the total energy is conserved with high accuracy (relative error $\lesssim 10^{-5}$).
L001680	
L001681	To identify the shock time, we monitor the FES on a log–log scale at successive times. At the shock time, the spectrum develops an inertial range with scaling $E_k \sim k^{-8/3}$ and a sharp  ``knee'' at its right edge. Before the shock time this knee is rounded, while after it evolves into a hump that collects the energy of short-wavelength solitons generated by the lattice dispersion.
L001682	
L001683	\begin{figure}[h!]
L001684	    \includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{Before2.pdf} \includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{AtShock2.pdf}
L001685	
L001686	    \includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{AfterShock2.pdf} \includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{Mixed2.pdf}
L001687	
L001688	    \caption{Fourier Energy spectrum of the FPUT chain with random initial data in a short interval around the shock time. In the first panel, the blue line represents the FES at $\sim 97\%$ shock time; in the second panel it is represented the FES at the shock time and in the third panel the FES at $\sim 103\%$ shock time. In the last panel we superimpose the three FES.}
L001689	\end{figure}
L001690	
L001691	To better highlight the power-law scaling, we reduce noise by computing the maximum of $E_k$ over windows in $k$ of width 10, and refer to the resulting curve as the ``filtered spectrum''. This filtering step is essential to determine the shock time accurately.
L001692	
L001693	\begin{figure}[h!]
L001694	    \begin{center}
L001695	    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{Filtrato-e-non.pdf}
L001696	    \caption{Filtered and bare FES at the shock time.}
L001697	    \end{center}
L001698	\end{figure}
L001699	
L001700	 For each data point in Fig.~\ref{FigLog}, we sampled $1000$ random initial conditions for a system with $2^{14}=16\,384$ masses. We let the system evolve and on a certain time-window (which depends on the number of excited modes) we computed the FES at any time and we plotted it. We then estimated the shock time using the procedure described above. The plotted values are the averages over these realizations at fixed $p$, the number of initially excited modes.
